    "summary": "Primal Other One jam and tight NFA>GDTRFB>NFA sequence",
    "blurb": "The show features a ferocious The Other One extended jam and a crisp Not Fade Away > Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad > Not Fade Away sequence with Phil’s bass front‑and‑center. Jerry delivers a notable Loser solo while the band maintains high energy throughout.",
    "review": "Winterland on May 29, 1971 finds the Dead in peak psychedelic form. After an early set of rockers like It Hurts Me Too and Hard To Handle, the second half erupts with a primal The Other One that showcases Phil Lesh’s thunderous, forward‑mixed bass and a searing Jerry Garcia solo on Loser. The momentum rolls straight into a tight Not Fade Away > Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad > Not Fade Away medley that feels both crisp and expansive, underscored by Bill Kreutzmann’s steady drumming and Pigpen’s subtle organ textures. Throughout the night the band rides a high‑energy current, delivering solid renditions of Wharf Rat and a spirited close with Not Fade Away.\n\nFor listeners seeking the best audio experience, Charlie Miller’s soundboard transfer (gd1971-05-29.sbd.miller.110324.flac16) captures every nuance – from Phil’s fat bass to the crystal‑clear guitar tones – making this performance as vivid today as it was in the room. While an audience tape also exists, its muffled mids and occasional dropouts make the Miller SBD the clear choice for reliving this iconic Winterland night.",
